# Publishing HTML artifacts

When the user asks to publish, share, or upload an HTML file, use the publisher script.

## Required environment variables

- `HTML_PUBLISHER_URL` — Worker base URL (e.g. `https://artifacts.example.com`)
- `HTML_PUBLISHER_TOKEN` — API bearer token

If either is missing, tell the user what to export and stop.

## Steps

1. Confirm the file starts with `<!doctype html` or `<html`, and is self-contained (the viewer blocks all network requests). Images, video, audio, fonts, PDFs and other binary files, and any file over 256 KB, are stored in R2 instead of Fossil (the CLI uploads them automatically, up to 100 MB each); smaller text files must compress under about 1.99 MB.
2. Run (a directory publishes a multi-file page):
   ```bash
   node scripts/publish-html.mjs --json --title "<title>" <file.html | dir>
   ```
3. Capture `viewerUrl` (`/v/<id>#<readToken>`), `editUrl` (`/v/<id>#<writeToken>`) and `cloneUrl` (`https://editor:<writeToken>@<host>/r/<id>`, for `fossil clone`/`push`) from the JSON output.
4. Return `viewerUrl` to the user. Keep `editUrl` and `cloneUrl` to yourself for later updates.

**Always post the link.** Every time you publish *or update* an artifact, give the user its read link: `viewerUrl` from the CLI output, on its own line, with the full `#…` token. It opens the newest version, and the CLI has already checked that it opens (`linkVerified: true`). Never post the edit link or clone URL in its place, and never build a link by hand. If `linkVerified` is false, say the link didn't open instead of presenting it as working.

Diagrams: write them in Pikchr inside `<pre class="pikchr" aria-label="...">` unless the user asks for another tool; the viewer renders them. `publish` and `update` refuse a page whose Pikchr doesn't render. Fonts: Instrument Sans by default (IBM Plex Mono for code, IBM Plex Serif for long reading); name them in CSS and the viewer embeds only those.
5. Surface any non-empty `warningMessages` from JSON output.

## Update

Each publish or update is a versioned check-in. To revise an artifact, update it at the same URL instead of publishing a new one:

```bash
node scripts/publish-html.mjs update --json "<editUrl>" <file.html | dir> --message "<what changed>"
```

It needs the edit link (a read link fails). Exit code 3 means a version conflict (someone published since; nothing saved): rerun to build on the newer version. Exit code 1 "nothing changed" means the files match the latest version. `history <url>` lists versions; `list` shows your artifacts.

## Key rules

- **Never strip the `#fragment`** from `viewerUrl`. It is the access token; without it the link cannot open the artifact.
- **`--delete-local`** only for files the agent generated in the current session when the user explicitly asks for cleanup. Never for user-owned source files.
- If upload fails, leave the file untouched and report the raw error.
- **Keep `editUrl` and `cloneUrl` private.** They can update and delete the artifact. Never print them into public places (PRs, issues, commits, channels, logs).

## TTL options

`--ttl 1h | 6h | 24h | 7d | 30d | never` (default: `7d`)

## Delete

```bash
node scripts/publish-html.mjs delete "<editUrl>"
```
